{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdWwVuReJU4pvxE33u18","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017YzXDFEjkKHZZX2uyg1Ejm","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"A":{"scope":5,"conventions":4,"tests":5,"readability":4,"notes":"Wraps the output of a macro without `:sections` in HTML-comment markers when that output contains a heading. `parse_sections` tracks marker depth and skips numbering headings between markers. A `@macro_depth` counter keeps the markers through nested `textilizable` calls (for example, `include` inside `collapse`) and removes them only at the outermost level. This handles nested macros correctly. Tests cover a plain include, an include nested in `collapse`, custom macros with and without `:sections`, alignment with `get_section`, and the `macro_sections?` helper. Drawbacks: the combined `SECTION_HEADING_RE` shifts the capture groups, and the `@macro_depth` state adds some complexity, though both are commented."},"B":{"scope":5,"conventions":4,"tests":4,"readability":4,"notes":"Replaces the output of a macro without `:sections` with a random placeholder comment and puts the output back after `parse_sections` numbers the headings. The idea is simple. However, it only hides output when the current `textilizable` call has `edit_section_links`. When `include` sits inside a `collapse` block, the inner `textilizable` call made by `collapse` has no `edit_section_links`, so the included headings are not hidden. The outer `parse_sections` then counts them and the section numbers shift again. The state also lives in an instance hash shared across calls. The test is good for the flat case: it checks section numbers against `get_section`, including headings inside `collapse`. It does not cover the nested case, which is exactly where the bug is. It adds a test that an invalid macro option raises an error."},"merge_first":"A","why":"A handles `include` nested inside `collapse` correctly and tests it. B misses that case: the inner `textilizable` call has no `edit_section_links`, so the included headings are counted again and the section numbers shift. Both changes have similar scope and clarity."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15281,"cache_read_input_tokens":13119,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15281},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ac1c1ca2-b78c-48da-9ba9-78f1a5a5725b","uuid":"b23908fe-1c46-4057-9d07-64b0d4092c20","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:04:12.573Z","request_id":"req_011CgdWwVgH3BvKBCj8wTR3f","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017YzXDFEjkKHZZX2uyg1Ejm":{"A":{"scope":5,"conventions":4,"tests":5,"readability":4,"notes":"Wraps the output of a macro without `:sections` in HTML-comment markers when that output contains a heading. `parse_sections` tracks marker depth and skips numbering headings between markers. A `@macro_depth` counter keeps the markers through nested `textilizable` calls (for example, `include` inside `collapse`) and removes them only at the outermost level. This handles nested macros correctly. Tests cover a plain include, an include nested in `collapse`, custom macros with and without `:sections`, alignment with `get_section`, and the `macro_sections?` helper. Drawbacks: the combined `SECTION_HEADING_RE` shifts the capture groups, and the `@macro_depth` state adds some complexity, though both are commented."},"B":{"scope":5,"conventions":4,"tests":4,"readability":4,"notes":"Replaces the output of a macro without `:sections` with a random placeholder comment and puts the output back after `parse_sections` numbers the headings. The idea is simple. However, it only hides output when the current `textilizable` call has `edit_section_links`. When `include` sits inside a `collapse` block, the inner `textilizable` call made by `collapse` has no `edit_section_links`, so the included headings are not hidden. The outer `parse_sections` then counts them and the section numbers shift again. The state also lives in an instance hash shared across calls. The test is good for the flat case: it checks section numbers against `get_section`, including headings inside `collapse`. It does not cover the nested case, which is exactly where the bug is. It adds a test that an invalid macro option raises an error."},"merge_first":"A","why":"A handles `include` nested inside `collapse` correctly and tests it. B misses that case: the inner `textilizable` call has no `edit_section_links`, so the included headings are counted again and the section numbers shift. Both changes have similar scope and clarity."}}}
